  clock: 2  # 910e
  nonce: 910e  # 910e
  set:  # 910e
    description: "Follow-up to WI-gitad (HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES drift guard, Part 1, shipped in PR #3950). Part 1 caught the *phantom-entry* direction: every entry in the `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES` frozenset must exist in at least one `io_primitives/*.yaml` catalog. This item adds Part 2 — the *missing-entry* direction.\n\n# What's the failure mode this guards against?\n\nToday, if a contributor adds a new subprocess-launching primitive to a YAML catalog (for example, adds `os.startfile` to `python.yaml` so taint analysis can track it), but forgets to also add it to `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES` in `io_boundary.py`, the analyzer will silently never flag calls to that primitive as `has_high_risk=True` in its JSON output. The catalogs know the primitive is a subprocess launcher; the high-risk classification just never fires. There is no warning. Nothing in CI catches this.\n\nPart 2 makes that scenario fail CI with a clear message naming the unclassified primitive.\n\n# What's the scope?\n\nLook at the kind-by-kind breakdown of every catalogued primitive vs. what's in `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES`:\n\n| `IoPrimitive.kind` | total catalog entries | in HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES | gap |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `subprocess`     | 127 | 50 | **77 missing** |\n| `fs_write`       | 419 | 6  | intentionally tight (only destructive ops flagged) |\n| `net_send`       | 270 | 5  | intentionally tight (only low-level socket ops flagged) |\n| (all other kinds) | various | 0 | n/a |\n\nThe `subprocess` row is the only one where the gap is \"stuff we probably forgot to flag\" rather than \"stuff we intentionally don't flag.\"\n\nConcretely the 77 missing subprocess primitives are almost certainly language coverage gaps — Kotlin/Scala/Elixir/Erlang/Haskell/Swift subprocess entries were added to catalogs (WI-rujos, WI-vibur, WI-zojid, etc.) after `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES` was originally written for Python/Go/Java/Rust/JS/C, and nothing closed the loop.\n\n**Recommended scope:** gate the test on `kind == \"subprocess\"` only. Don't try to gate `fs_write` or `net_send` — the catalog→frozenset gap there is by design and an exemption list would be enormous and unhelpful.\n\n# What's the implementation plan?\n\nThis is a three-step PR, doable in one sitting:\n\n**Step 1 — Reconcile the 77 missing subprocess entries.** Run the helper below to dump the missing entries, then for each one decide: add to `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES`, or add to a new `HIGH_RISK_EXEMPTIONS_SUBPROCESS: frozenset[str]` in `io_boundary.py` with a comment explaining why it's exempt. Expect most to go into HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES — these are subprocess.run-equivalents in other languages and they should be flagged. The exemption set should be small (probably <10 entries).\n\nHelper to enumerate the gap:\n\n```python\nfrom hypergumbo_core.io_boundary import HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES, load_catalog\n\nlanguages = [\"python\", \"go\", \"java\", \"rust\", \"javascript\", \"c\", \"cpp\",\n             \"elixir\", \"erlang\", \"kotlin\", \"scala\", \"swift\", \"objc\", \"haskell\"]\nmissing: list[tuple[str, str]] = []  # (qualified_name, source language)\nfor lang in languages:\n    for p in load_catalog(lang).primitives:\n        if p.kind == \"subprocess\" and p.qualified_name not in HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES:\n            missing.append((p.qualified_name, lang))\nfor q, lang in sorted(missing):\n    print(f\"  {q}  ({lang})\")\n```\n\n**Step 2 — Write the test.** Add `test_every_subprocess_kind_primitive_is_classified` to `packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_io_boundary.py`, in the same `TestHighRiskPrimitivesDriftGuard` class that holds Part 1.\n\nThe test must emit a **reader-friendly assertion message** when it fires — see the \"What the test failure looks like\" section below for the required shape. The shape matters because this message IS the human-facing interface of the drift guard. The implementation must produce it verbatim (or a strict superset). Reviewers should reject a PR whose message is just `assert not unclassified` or some other terse form.\n\nCode skeleton:\n\n```python\ndef test_every_subprocess_kind_primitive_is_classified(self) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Part 2: every kind=subprocess catalog entry must be in\n    HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES or in HIGH_RISK_EXEMPTIONS_SUBPROCESS.\"\"\"\n    unclassified: list[tuple[str, str]] = []  # (qualified_name, source language)\n    for lang in CATALOG_LANGUAGES:\n        for p in load_catalog(lang).primitives:\n            if p.kind != \"subprocess\":\n                continue\n            q = p.qualified_name\n            if q in HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES or q in HIGH_RISK_EXEMPTIONS_SUBPROCESS:\n                continue\n            unclassified.append((q, lang))\n    assert not unclassified, _format_failure_message(unclassified)\n```\n\n…where `_format_failure_message` produces the shape spec'd below. `CATALOG_LANGUAGES` already exists in the Part 1 test — reuse it.\n\n**Step 3 — Update CHANGELOG and close this item.** One-liner under \"### Added\" naming Part 2 and this WI ID.\n\n# What the test failure looks like (REQUIRED — this is the spec, not a suggestion)\n\nWhen the test fails, the developer sees this in their CI log or local pytest output. The message must include all five elements below, in this order:\n\n```\nHIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES drift guard (Part 2, WI-sugav): 3 subprocess-kind\nprimitives are present in io_primitives YAML catalogs but not classified\nin io_boundary.py.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS:\n  A primitive with kind=subprocess is a process-launching API\n  (subprocess.Popen, Runtime.exec, os.system, and friends). We want every\n  such primitive to have an explicit \"high-risk\" classification so the\n  has_high_risk=True flag fires consistently when a user analyzes a\n  repo that calls into one. Each unclassified entry below is silently\n  falling through — the catalog tracks it for taint analysis, but the\n  high-risk display flag never fires.\n\nUNCLASSIFIED PRIMITIVES:\n  kotlin.lang.Runtime.exec         (kotlin.yaml)\n  scala.sys.process.Process.apply  (scala.yaml)\n  elixir.System.cmd                (elixir.yaml)\n\nHOW TO FIX:\n  Edit packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/io_boundary.py and,\n  for each primitive above, pick ONE of:\n\n  (a) Add to HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES (the common case).\n      Pick this if the primitive launches an arbitrary subprocess\n      — i.e., calls of the shape \"run this command string with these\n      args, give me the result.\" This is what subprocess.Popen,\n      Runtime.exec, os.system, and Process.spawn all do.\n\n  (b) Add to HIGH_RISK_EXEMPTIONS_SUBPROCESS with an inline comment\n      explaining why (the rare case). Pick this if the primitive is\n      technically kind=subprocess in the catalog but doesn't represent\n      arbitrary code execution — e.g., it's a thin wrapper around a\n      single known utility (`git`-only, `docker`-only) that's already\n      classified elsewhere.\n\n  After editing, re-run `pytest packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_io_boundary.py::TestHighRiskPrimitivesDriftGuard`\n  and the test should pass.\n\nSee WI-sugav (tracker item) for the design rationale.\n```\n\nFive required elements:\n1. **What broke**, one sentence with the count and a name for the rule. Include the WI ID so a future developer can find the design doc from the error alone.\n2. **WHY THIS MATTERS**, two-to-three sentences. Don't assume the reader knows what `has_high_risk` is or what `kind=subprocess` means.\n3. **UNCLASSIFIED PRIMITIVES**, one per line, with the source catalog in parens. Sort alphabetically by qualified_name. (Including the source catalog answers \"where did this come from?\" without making the dev grep.)\n4. **HOW TO FIX**, with the absolute file path, both options (a) and (b), and the decision rule for picking between them. The decision rule must use concrete examples so a dev who's never thought about taint analysis can still classify correctly.\n5. The exact `pytest` command to re-run, so the fix→verify loop is one copy-paste.\n\nTwo anti-patterns the implementer should NOT ship:\n\n- A single-line assert like `assert not unclassified, f\"missing: {unclassified}\"`. That leaves the dev to figure out which file to edit, what the options are, and how to decide between them.\n- Boilerplate `pytest.fail(\"...\")` with no decision rule. The \"is it (a) or (b)?\" question is the actual work; the message has to scaffold it.\n\n# What does the exemption list look like?\n\nSame shape as `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES`: a `frozenset[str]` of qualified names. Defined right below `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES` in `io_boundary.py`, with a comment that explains the semantic: \"These are kind=subprocess in the catalogs (so taint analysis tracks them) but intentionally NOT high-risk for display purposes — e.g., because they wrap a higher-level command that the catalog already classifies elsewhere.\" Each entry should have a short inline comment with the specific reason.\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `test_every_subprocess_kind_primitive_is_classified` exists and passes on dev.\n2. The test's assertion message matches the spec in \"What the test failure looks like\" above (verified by a unit test that calls the formatter with a synthetic 2-entry input and asserts the output shape).\n3. `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES` has been augmented with whichever of the 77 missing entries belong there.\n4. `HIGH_RISK_EXEMPTIONS_SUBPROCESS` exists, even if empty, so future additions have an obvious home.\n5. CHANGELOG `[Unreleased]` has a one-line entry under \"### Added\" naming Part 2 and this WI ID.\n6. The full suite passes locally with 100% coverage on the new code path.\n\n# What this does NOT include\n\n- Doesn't touch `fs_write` or `net_send` gating — those are intentionally tighter than the catalog, and trying to enumerate exemptions would be a maintenance burden with no payoff.\n- Doesn't change `has_high_risk` semantics or any downstream consumer — purely tightens the coverage of the existing flag.\n- Doesn't refactor `HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES` into something derived-from-catalog. That's a different (much larger) refactor.\n\n# Why P4\n\nSame priority as the parent (WI-gitad). HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES is display-only — it doesn't affect taint-flow correctness, only what gets surfaced as \"this is dangerous\" in JSON output. Low impact on analysis quality, modest impact on user-facing risk surfacing."  # 910e
