  clock: 5  # aa9c
  nonce: aa9c  # aa9c
  message: "Shipped in PR #3954 (merged 2026-05-28). \n  \nHIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES extended by 48 unique qualified names spanning all 14 catalog languages; new HIGH_RISK_EXEMPTIONS_SUBPROCESS frozenset adds 18 entries for wait/signal/PATH-lookup/self-exit primitives. New drift guard test_every_subprocess_boundary_primitive_is_classified runs alongside Part 1's phantom-detection test in TestHighRiskPrimitivesDriftGuard.\n\nAcceptance criteria status:\n1. ✓ test_every_subprocess_boundary_primitive_is_classified exists and passes on dev.\n2. ✓ The test's assertion message matches the WI-sugav spec verbatim, locked by test_subprocess_drift_message_formatter_shape (5 required structural elements: header with WI ID, WHY THIS MATTERS, sorted UNCLASSIFIED PRIMITIVES with source catalog in parens, HOW TO FIX with absolute path and (a)/(b) decision rule, exact pytest re-run command).\n3. ✓ HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES augmented with the missing entries that represent arbitrary execution.\n4. ✓ HIGH_RISK_EXEMPTIONS_SUBPROCESS exists (18 entries — wait/signal/PATH-lookup/self-exit).\n5. ✓ CHANGELOG [Unreleased] under Added.\n6. ✓ Full suite (14342 tests) passed with 100% coverage on changed source files.\n\nTwo minor improvements beyond the spec:\n- The drift-guard message uses 'boundary=subprocess' (the actual field) rather than 'kind=subprocess' (which was an error in the spec — IoPrimitive.kind is function/method/attribute, the boundary classification field is IoPrimitive.boundary).\n- Added test_high_risk_and_exemption_sets_are_disjoint so an entry can't accidentally be classified in both sets and silently bypass the gate; added test_subprocess_drift_message_empty_input_is_well_formed so the formatter can't regress on max() of an empty iterable."  # aa9c
